docs:
Installed `Stepper` and `useAutoPlay` (`@/components/ui/stepper`) and the framework-free core they use (`@/components/ui/stepper-core`).

```tsx
import { Stepper } from "@/components/ui/stepper";

const [active, setActive] = useState(0);

<Stepper count={5} active={active} onStepClick={setActive} />
```

Autoplay, with a pause control:

```tsx
import { Stepper, useAutoPlay } from "@/components/ui/stepper";

const { playing, toggle, filling, fillDuration } = useAutoPlay({
  count: 5,
  active,
  onStepChange: setActive,
  stepDuration: 3000,
});

<Stepper count={5} active={active} filling={filling} fillDuration={fillDuration} />
<button onClick={toggle}>{playing ? "Pause" : "Play"}</button>
```

The styles live in the component file; React 19 hoists them into `<head>` once, so no CSS import is needed. Theme it with the item's variables (`--stepper-bg`, `--stepper-active-bg`, `--stepper-fill-bg`, `--stepper-container-bg`, `--stepper-container-border`, `--stepper-container-radius`, `--stepper-dot-size`, `--stepper-active-width`, `--stepper-gap`, `--stepper-focus-ring`), added to your theme for light and dark, or per instance through `className` or `style` (for example `className="[--stepper-active-bg:var(--primary)]"`). Windowing (`maxVisible`) assumes the default dot, pill and gap sizes.

Ported from Josh Puckett's fluid stepper (MIT); the license notice is at the end of every file.

